\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine c.bank buys dollars for 2nd day  \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2006\par 17:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (adds details, more comment)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Sept 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday, buying dollars at 5.0 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second day in a row, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "The situation was clear from the morning. There were several large dollar sellers," a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 "The funds are coming in from exporters and investors. We do not see many large import purchases."  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said most deals were concluded at about 5.0080/5.0130.  \par \par \uc2 "We can make a forecast for about a month ahead that the market will stay at about these levels," another dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar supply and demand will either be balanced or more hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be sold. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to sell dollars at the moment. That could happen closer to the end of the year when we see what the price of gas will be."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps a close watch over the market and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently, both buying and selling hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  within 5.00-5.06. It has defended these levels for more than a year.  \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's export-led economy is expected to grow this year and next. But a threat to macroeconomic and financial stability comes from uncertainty over prices for Russian natural gas. Kiev agreed to a nearly two-fold increase this year, but further rises are expected next year.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Mykola Azarov has said gas prices could rise to at least $135 per 1,000 cubic metres from $95 at the moment. But the government has yet to clinch a final deal with Russia for 2007.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060912e29c001db\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
