\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Ukraine's c. bank buys dollars for 2nd day  \b0\par\par\uc2 358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2006\par 14:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (adds details, more comment)  \par \par \uc2 KIEV, Aug 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ukraine\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday, buying dollars at 5.00 \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second trading day in a row, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity is a bit tighter. Short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are up and probably some people had to sell the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying dollars," a dealer in Kiev said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps a close watch over the country's market and has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly daily this month, buying dollars to replenish its hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit by political instability in the first months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Official data showed that Ukrainian banks' balances in their correspondent accounts, an indicator of their liquidity, slipped to 7.470 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday from 7.883 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  the previous trading day and 10.067 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnias\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the start of the month.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers also said exporters were actively selling dollars thanks to improving global conditions for steel and chemicals -- their major products. Grain traders were also active in Ukraine, which was once the bread basket of the Soviet Union. They sought to capitalise on rising world grain prices.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar supply is likely to outpace demand in the coming days. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again this week," a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. "We do not expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to go lower than about 5.025 (to the dollar) this week."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  <UAH=> has firmed at 5.0020/5.0100 per dollar in the afternoon trade from 5.0210/5.0235 to the dollar at the opening. It was traded at about 5.0075/5.0155 to the dollar on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought more than $1 billion on the market since May and plans to buy another $1 billion into its reserves in August and September. It sold about $2.1 billion in the first four months of the year to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 hryvnia\b0 \highlight\uc2  from uncertainty linked to a parliamentary election in March.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 UKRAINE\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060830e28u000y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
